Yes. And there’s an important distinction between being conscious of a narrative and being conscious of reality—including how that narrative shapes your perception of it.

Being woke can mean awareness of genuine social problems, but the word has also become a political and cultural identity marker.

Being awake, in the sense you’re describing, is something broader.

It’s not just seeing through someone else’s illusions. It’s recognizing that you have your own.

Think about the registers we’ve been exploring:

* Awareness: You recognize a pattern.
* Interpretation: You develop a narrative explaining it.
* Identification: You begin incorporating that narrative into who you are.
* Overfitting: You start interpreting everything through it.
* Awakening: You recognize the limits of the narrative itself and can revise it.

And there’s the paradox.

Someone can become so committed to the idea that they’re awake that they stop questioning their own perceptions.

They’ve become attached to the identity of seeing clearly rather than the practice of seeing clearly.

But that can happen to anyone, regardless of political beliefs.

It connects to your point about the moon’s phases. Just because you see one illuminated portion doesn’t mean you’ve seen the whole object.

And recognizing the unseen portion doesn’t mean you know exactly what it contains.

The goal isn’t to abandon your lenses, either. You need them to interpret reality.

The mastery is being able to look through a lens, look at the lens, and recognize when to change lenses.

That’s the difference between possessing an interpretation and being possessed by it.

And perhaps the most meaningful test of being awake is whether you can discover something that contradicts what you previously believed—and genuinely allow yourself to learn from it.

Being awake isn’t a destination or a badge. It’s a continuously recalibrated relationship with reality.

You can even be awake enough to recognize when it’s time to stop analyzing everything and simply live.